{-
   The CFG cleanup, and the three shapes it has to be right about - `compiler/resolve_ssa/opt/cfg.cpp`.

   Every claim here is a **number**, deliberately. A branch folded to the wrong arm, an edge threaded
   to the destination the other constant would have selected and a `Drop` deleted out of a block that
   is still reachable are all invisible in the emitted bytes, and all three change what this program
   answers. The optimized/unoptimized run comparison is what turns each of them into a failure;
   `.ssa.opt.expect` beside this is what says which shape the pass actually left behind.

   `live` is the balance and `trace` is the order, which is the pair every ownership fixture here
   uses: a teardown that ran twice takes the first negative and one that never ran leaves it
   positive, and neither of those shows up in a digit of the second.
-}

let &live = 0 :: Int
let &trace = 0 :: Int

data Held {id: Int}

instance Drop(Held):
    fn drop(->self: Held):
        live = live - 1
        trace = trace * 10 + self.id

fn held(id: Int) -> Held:
    live = live + 1
    return Held {id: id}

fn take(->h: Held) -> Int = h.id

-- A branch on a condition the source wrote, with an owned value made and released inside the arm
-- that goes away. What the fold has to be right about is not the number this answers but the
-- teardown it deletes with the block: a `Drop` in a block nothing reaches runs never, so `live` and
-- `trace` are what say the arm was stranded rather than executed.
fn written(n: Int) -> Int:
    if False:
        let h = held(9)
        return h.id + n

    return n + 1

-- The same fold on a condition nothing wrote. `simplify` answers the arithmetic and the comparison,
-- and `cleanCfg` is what the constant then reaches - which is the order the driver runs the two in
-- and the reason most constant conditions are made rather than written.
fn made(n: Int) -> Int:
    if 3 - 3 > 0:
        let h = held(8)
        return h.id + n

    return n + 2

{-
   An owned local in an `iter fn` with a `break` out of its loop, handed over on one path out and
   not on the other.

   That is the recipe for the only continuation region this tier has. The hand-over is conditional,
   so the frame carries a flag and the teardown is a `Cleanup` region behind a test of it, and the
   rule that region states - an edge may enter it at its `entry` and nowhere else - is what a
   reroute around the flag test would break. `Continuation::multiplicity` is the other half: a
   cleanup arm runs at most once per entry, so an edge from inside it back to its first block is a
   teardown running as many times as the loop does.

   [[yana-resolve-cfg-invariants]] is why the `break` is here rather than a plain fall-out. The
   block a `break` leaves through holds a jump and nothing else, so splicing it points its
   predecessor straight at the loop's exit - which *moves* an edge, and moving one is what put the
   shipping stage's block list out of reverse postorder and produced a wrong number with no
   diagnostic. Nothing here owes that debt, because the lowering takes the shared reverse postorder
   rather than the table order, and this is the fixture that says so.
-}
iter fn steps(n: Int) -> Int:
    let &h = held(n)
    let &i = 0 :: Int

    while i < n:
        if i > 2:
            yield take(h)
            break

        yield i
        i = i + 1

-- The consumer, whose own `break` is a second one: the `for` body is a continuation region at the
-- MIR tier and an outlined function at this one, so what a pass sees here is one loop driving
-- another through a call it cannot see into.
fn nested(n: Int) -> Int:
    let &sum = 0 :: Int

    for x in steps(n):
        if x > 4:
            break

        sum = sum * 10 + x

    return sum

-- A boolean computed, joined, and then branched on, which is the shape `threadBranches` is written
-- for: the arm that handed the join a constant is pointed straight at the destination that constant
-- selects. Every combination is exercised below, and the three answers are distinct, so an edge
-- threaded to the wrong arm is a different total rather than a different dump.
fn joined(a: Bool, b: Bool, c: Bool) -> Int:
    let ok = (a && b) || c
    if ok then return 1
    return 2

{-
   A join every predecessor decides the same way, which is the thread this pass has to **refuse**.

   Both sides of the inner `if` hand the join `True`, so every edge into it goes round and the join
   goes with them - and the `else` of the outer `if` is then a block no edge names. An unreachable
   block is still checked structurally and nothing dominates one, so every operand it reads is
   reported at `threadBranches`'s own checkpoint; what would have to go with the arm is everything
   only that arm reached, which is `cleanCfg`'s sweep and not something a thread may perform on its
   way past. So the whole thread is declined and the diamond is left standing.

   The number is what says the refusal did not also lose the answer, which is the failure the other
   direction would have: `always` is `n + 1` on every path a program can take, and the arm this
   leaves in place is the one nothing reaches.
-}
fn always(c: Bool, n: Int) -> Int:
    let ok = if c then True else True

    if ok:
        return n + 1

    return n + 2

fn main() -> Int:
    let &total = 0

    total = total + written(10) * 1
    total = total + made(10) * 2

    total = total + nested(0) * 3
    total = total + nested(3) * 5
    total = total + nested(6) * 7

    total = total + joined(True, True, True) * 11
    total = total + joined(True, True, False) * 13
    total = total + joined(True, False, True) * 17
    total = total + joined(True, False, False) * 19
    total = total + joined(False, True, True) * 23
    total = total + joined(False, True, False) * 29
    total = total + joined(False, False, True) * 31
    total = total + joined(False, False, False) * 37

    total = total + always(True, 5) * 41
    total = total + always(False, 5) * 43

    return total * 1000 + trace * 10 + live
